plot(pca1, choix = "ind", habillage = 4, label = "none",
     title = "Shape PCA: Origin")
plot(pca1, choix = "var", habillage = 4)
```

We check redundancy numerically rather than relying on the PCA alone.

```{r redundancy}
# Compare ratios and test whether the third is the product of the first two.
shape_cor <- cor(d1[shape_names])
shape_cor
redundancy_error <- max(abs(d1$rEV31 - d1$rEV21 * d1$rEV32))
redundancy_error
```

**Correlation table:** Rows and columns identify measurements. Their
intersection ranges from -1 to +1: positive means increasing together,
negative means moving oppositely, and near zero means little linear relation.
The diagonal is 1; the two halves repeat the same comparisons.

**Answer:** PC3 captures only `r round(pca1$eig[3, 2], 2)`%; the first two PCs
retain `r round(pca1$eig[2, 3], 2)`%. The ratios repeat information because
\(rEV31=rEV21\times rEV32\), with maximum discrepancy
`r format(redundancy_error, scientific = TRUE, digits = 3)`, consistent with
rounding. PC3 is not exactly zero because this relation is multiplicative,
while PCA captures linear variation. RNA and protein points overlap widely;
shape does not clearly separate Origins.

