## Bootstrap stability: k = 4, then 2 and 3

Bootstrap asks whether groups survive a changed sample. We draw 600 indices
with replacement, rebuild Ward, and match each original group to the new group
with maximum **Jaccard overlap**: shared members divided by all distinct members.
It ranges from 0 (no overlap) to 1 (identical sets).

We compare only sampled identities, counting duplicates once in the overlap
sets. This avoids penalizing absent pockets. All copies still enter clustering.
Each group gets its best match; matching is not forced to be one-to-one.

```{r bootstrap-clusters}
# Run 500 reproducible resamples at each k.
B <- 500
set.seed(22518830)
stability <- list()
stability_table <- data.frame()
for (k in c(4, 2, 3)) {
  original <- cutree(tree3, k = k)
  scores <- matrix(NA_real_, B, k)
  for (b in 1:B) {
    # Resample and rebuild groups in the same shape space.
    ids <- sample(1:nrow(x3), nrow(x3), replace = TRUE)
    bootstrap_tree <- hclust(dist(x3[ids, ]), method = "ward.D2")
    bootstrap_groups <- cutree(bootstrap_tree, k = k)
    present <- unique(ids)
    for (g in 1:k) {
      reference <- intersect(which(original == g), present)
      if (length(reference) == 0) next
      matches <- numeric(k)
      for (h in 1:k) {
        # Calculate overlap with each candidate group.
        candidate <- unique(ids[bootstrap_groups == h])
        shared <- length(intersect(reference, candidate))
        total <- length(union(reference, candidate))
        matches[h] <- shared / total
      }
      scores[b, g] <- max(matches)
    }
  }
  # Average each group's best overlap across resamples.
  mean_jaccard <- colMeans(scores, na.rm = TRUE)
  stability[[as.character(k)]] <- mean_jaccard
  stability_table <- rbind(stability_table,
    data.frame(k = k, Cluster = 1:k, Mean_Jaccard = mean_jaccard))
}
stability_table
aggregate(Mean_Jaccard ~ k, data = stability_table, FUN = mean)
```

**Columns:** `k` identifies the partition; `Cluster` is its original group;
`Mean_Jaccard` is its mean best overlap over 500 resamples. The final table
averages across groups, weighting each group equally. Missing comparisons
are omitted if a group has no sampled member.

**Answer:** k = 4 scores range from `r round(min(stability[["4"]]), 3)` to
`r round(max(stability[["4"]]), 3)`, showing instability. k = 2 gives
`r paste(round(stability[["2"]], 3), collapse = " and ")`, better but limited.
k = 3 gives `r paste(round(stability[["3"]], 3), collapse = ", ")`.
None reaches the usual 0.75 mean-Jaccard stability guideline. We retain four
groups in session 4 only for the requested comparison.

## Session 3 summary

**Same question?** No. Silhouette measures observed separation; bootstrap
measures reproducibility under resampling. Both favor k = 2 here, but neither
strongly supports clearly separated natural groups.

# Session 4 — Clustering, PCA, and heatmap

## Repeat shape clustering and add scaled shape-plus-size clustering

We compare the same pockets at k = 4. The first tree exactly repeats the
previous unscaled ratios; the second adds size and therefore requires scaling.

```{r two-clusterings}
