# Reuse the five-variable tree and retain the stated column order.
pheatmap(z4, cluster_rows = tree_five, cluster_cols = FALSE,
         annotation_row = annotation, annotation_colors = annotation_colors,
         show_rownames = FALSE, border_color = NA,
         color = hcl.colors(100, "Blue-Red 3"), fontsize = 9)
```

**Do blocks line up?** Five-variable groups form contiguous blocks because
their own tree orders the rows. Shape-only groups are more mixed; Origin is
also mixed. Matching colors across the two group bars do not automatically
mean matching groups.

```{r clustering-agreement}
# Count shared members: shape groups in rows, five-variable groups in columns.
cross_table <- table(Shape = cluster_shape, Five = cluster_five)
cross_table

# Convert shared counts into Jaccard overlaps for every group pair.
jaccard_matrix <- matrix(0, 4, 4)
rownames(jaccard_matrix) <- paste("Shape", 1:4)
colnames(jaccard_matrix) <- paste("Five", 1:4)
for (i in 1:4) {
  for (j in 1:4) {
    shared <- cross_table[i, j]
    total <- sum(cross_table[i, ]) + sum(cross_table[, j]) - shared
    jaccard_matrix[i, j] <- shared / total
  }
}
jaccard_matrix

# Find the best match for each shape group.
best_matches <- data.frame(Shape_cluster = 1:4,
                          Best_five_cluster = integer(4),
                          Best_Jaccard = numeric(4))
for (i in 1:4) {
  best_matches$Best_five_cluster[i] <- which.max(jaccard_matrix[i, ])
  best_matches$Best_Jaccard[i] <- max(jaccard_matrix[i, ])
}
best_matches

# Compare the complete partitions using ARI.
comparison <- fpc::cluster.stats(dist_shape, cluster_shape, cluster_five,
                                 compareonly = TRUE)
agreement <- comparison$corrected.rand
agreement
```

**Tables:** The contingency entries count shared pockets. Jaccard entries
give shared membership divided by combined distinct membership, between 0
and 1. `Shape_cluster` identifies the old group, `Best_five_cluster` its
highest-overlap new group, and `Best_Jaccard` that overlap. Numbers are
arbitrary, so the contingency diagonal has no special meaning.

**Numerical answer:** Best overlaps are
`r paste(round(best_matches$Best_Jaccard, 3), collapse = ", ")`;
ARI is `r round(agreement, 3)`. Agreement is partial: adding size changes
membership, not just group names.

## Final summary connecting the sessions

Session 1 separated shape from size and demonstrated why scaling matters.
Session 2 showed that linkage changes memberships, with Ward avoiding strong
chaining. Session 3 found weak separation and limited reproducibility of four
shape groups. Session 4 showed that standardized size adds a different grouping
signal. Together, the results support overlapping shape variation plus size
variation, rather than four firmly established natural shape groups or two
completely separate Origins.

