## Archetype rule and comparison with both clusterings

**Limitation:** The complete CSV and detailed QMD named in `document.pdf`
are absent. If the CSV is unavailable, we reconstruct the article's rule;
these are not independently supplied labels. Archetypes are median-threshold
categories, not Ward results: high/high ratios give sphere-like, high/low
disk-like, low/high rod-like, and low/low strongly anisotropic shapes.

```{r archetype-rule}
# Estimate pooled thresholds and assign all four ratio combinations.
median21 <- median(d4$rEV21)
median32 <- median(d4$rEV32)
data.frame(Variable = c("rEV21", "rEV32"),
           Median_threshold = c(median21, median32))
high21 <- d4$rEV21 >= median21
high32 <- d4$rEV32 >= median32
rule_archetype <- rep("Strongly anisotropic", nrow(d4))
rule_archetype[high21 & high32] <- "Sphere-like"
rule_archetype[high21 & !high32] <- "Disk-like"
rule_archetype[!high21 & high32] <- "Rod-like"

# Load supplied labels if available, matching pocket identities first.
archetype <- rule_archetype
label_source <- "Reconstructed median-rule labels"
complete_path <- "../data/2026-09-09_data_PROT_RNA_TP_seance4_complet.csv"
if (file.exists(complete_path)) {
  complete <- read.csv(complete_path)
  key <- paste(d4$PDB_ID, d4$Pocket)
  complete_key <- paste(complete$PDB_ID, complete$Pocket)
  index <- match(key, complete_key)
  stopifnot("Archetype" %in% names(complete), !anyNA(index),
            !anyDuplicated(complete_key), !anyNA(complete$Archetype[index]))
  archetype <- complete$Archetype[index]
  label_source <- "Archetype labels loaded from the complete file"
}
label_source
```

`Variable` names each ratio; `Median_threshold` gives its dividing value.
High means at least the median. Identity matching avoids comparing unrelated
rows if the complete file has a different order.

```{r archetype-agreement}
# Compare each clustering with the rule; integer labels preserve membership.
archetype_labels <- as.integer(factor(archetype))
shape_comparison <- fpc::cluster.stats(dist_shape, cluster_shape,
                                      archetype_labels, compareonly = TRUE)
five_comparison <- fpc::cluster.stats(dist_shape, cluster_five,
                                     archetype_labels, compareonly = TRUE)
shape_archetype_ari <- shape_comparison$corrected.rand
five_archetype_ari <- five_comparison$corrected.rand
data.frame(Clustering = c("Three ratios", "Five variables"),
           ARI_with_Archetype = c(shape_archetype_ari, five_archetype_ari))
```

`Clustering` identifies the analysis; `ARI_with_Archetype` measures its agreement
with the categories. Shape-only ARI is `r round(shape_archetype_ari, 3)` versus
`r round(five_archetype_ari, 3)` with size. Neither reproduces the rule exactly;
shape-only agrees more because the rule excludes size.

## Frequencies and shape-versus-size effect sizes

```{r frequencies-effects}
# Compare category counts and percentages within each Origin.
frequency <- table(Archetype = archetype, Origin = d4$Origin)
frequency
100 * prop.table(frequency, margin = 2)

# Express each RNA-minus-protein mean difference in pooled SD units.
effect_table <- data.frame(Variable = five_names, RNA_mean = numeric(5),
                          PROT_mean = numeric(5), Cohen_d = numeric(5))
for (i in 1:5) {
  rna_values <- d4[d4$Origin == "RNA", five_names[i]]
  protein_values <- d4[d4$Origin == "PROT", five_names[i]]
  pooled_sd <- sqrt((var(rna_values) + var(protein_values)) / 2)
  effect_table$RNA_mean[i] <- mean(rna_values)
  effect_table$PROT_mean[i] <- mean(protein_values)
  effect_table$Cohen_d[i] <-
    (mean(rna_values) - mean(protein_values)) / pooled_sd
}
effect_table
```

**Tables:** Frequency rows are categories; columns are Origins. Percentages
divide each column by 300. `Variable` names a measurement; `RNA_mean` and
`PROT_mean` are original-unit averages; `Cohen_d` is their standardized
difference. Positive d means higher in RNA. Equal group sizes allow the
simple pooled-SD formula above.

**Article comparison:** Counts reproduce Table 3: sphere-like 49 RNA/86
protein, disk-like 91/74, strongly anisotropic 75/60, rod-like 85/80.
Globally, disk-like and rod-like each account for 27.5%, the others 22.5%.
Proteins have more sphere-like pockets; RNA has more disk-like and strongly
anisotropic pockets; rod-like frequencies are similar.

The d values reproduce Table 1: shape -0.165/-0.289/-0.372, atom count +0.894,
and `Rg` +0.412. Shape shifts are smaller than the atom-count difference.
RNA pockets are larger on average, while the shape landscape remains shared.

## Bootstrap stability of the median rule

Following the article, we resample 300 protein and 300 RNA pockets separately
1,000 times. Each time we recompute thresholds, classify **all original
pockets**, and record ARI and the fraction changing category.

```{r threshold-bootstrap}
# Prepare reproducible Origin-stratified samples and result vectors.
protein_ids <- which(d4$Origin == "PROT")
rna_ids <- which(d4$Origin == "RNA")
original_rule_labels <- as.integer(factor(rule_archetype))
B_rule <- 1000
set.seed(20260910)
rule_ari <- numeric(B_rule)
reclassified <- numeric(B_rule)
for (b in 1:B_rule) {
  # Estimate pooled medians from the resampled pockets.
  sampled_protein <- sample(protein_ids, length(protein_ids), replace = TRUE)
  sampled_rna <- sample(rna_ids, length(rna_ids), replace = TRUE)
  ids <- c(sampled_protein, sampled_rna)
  new_median21 <- median(d4$rEV21[ids])
  new_median32 <- median(d4$rEV32[ids])

  # Apply new boundaries to all 600 original pockets.
  high21 <- d4$rEV21 >= new_median21
  high32 <- d4$rEV32 >= new_median32
  new_labels <- rep("Strongly anisotropic", nrow(d4))
  new_labels[high21 & high32] <- "Sphere-like"
  new_labels[high21 & !high32] <- "Disk-like"
  new_labels[!high21 & high32] <- "Rod-like"
  comparison <- fpc::cluster.stats(dist_shape, original_rule_labels,
    as.integer(factor(new_labels)), compareonly = TRUE)
  rule_ari[b] <- comparison$corrected.rand
  reclassified[b] <- mean(new_labels != rule_archetype)
}

# Summarize agreement and percentage changing category.
bootstrap_summary <- rbind(
  ARI = quantile(rule_ari, c(0.025, 0.5, 0.975)),
  Reclassified_percent = 100 * quantile(reclassified, c(0.025, 0.5, 0.975)))
bootstrap_summary
```

**Columns:** `2.5%`--`97.5%` give the central 95% range; `50%` is the median.
Rows measure ARI agreement and percentage reclassified.

**Answer:** Median ARI is `r round(median(rule_ari), 3)`
[`r round(quantile(rule_ari, 0.025), 3)`, `r round(quantile(rule_ari, 0.975), 3)`];
`r round(100 * median(reclassified), 1)`% change category, close to the article's
0.92 [0.82, 0.98] and 3%. Stable medians give reproducible categories;
Ward groups were less stable. Their sampled-group Jaccard and the rule's
all-pocket ARI are different statistics. Stable thresholds do not prove
naturally separated groups.

**Source:** [Ziani et al. (2026)](https://doi.org/10.34133/csbj.0022), supplied
`article.pdf`, Tables 1 and 3 and bootstrap methods.
